before:
size() can inspect a const string. Calling a mutating operation such as clear() through this const reference is rejected.

On a mutable string, clear() takes no arguments, removes all characters and returns no value. This is separate from the const parameter below:
```cpp
std::string text{"Moon"};
text.clear();
std::cout << text.size() << "\n";
```
Output: 0.

Complete local C++ example; the app itself does not execute it:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
void show_size(const std::string& name)
{
    std::cout << name.size() << "\n";
}

int main()
{
    std::string topic{"Moon"};
    show_size(topic);
    std::cout << topic << "\n";
}

```

